## std::string — String / Str method surface, routed through FFI alias.
##
## #06.8 T#13: every method here was a `String_<m>` entry in
## `compiler/riven_core/src/codegen/runtime_table/mod.rs` until T#21's
## namespace-anchor mechanism made it possible to attach FFI alias
## decls directly to the builtin `Ty::String`. Bootstrap merge reuses
## the existing TypeAlias DefId for `String` (so `Ty::String` stays
## canonical for codegen) and pumps the class-body `lib` decls into
## `HirProgram.ffi_libs`. MIR's `ffi_alias_map` then rewrites the
## mangled `String_<m>` callee to the C symbol BEFORE codegen would
## ever consult `runtime_table`.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## The runtime C entrypoints in `library/runtime/core/string.c` take
## a `const char *` (the RivenString buffer pointer) and return
## `char *` / `int64_t` / `void *` etc. — all pointer-or-i64 sized,
## so they wire-match the Riven sigs declared below regardless of
## whether the receiver is declared as `Ty::Class { name: "String" }`
## (what `register_class_lib_method` prepends for instance methods) or
## the canonical `Ty::String`. The cranelift import sig and the call
## site agree at the wire level.
##
## ## Return type semantics
##
## Several methods have richer typeck return types than the wire C
## sig (`find` → `Option[USize]`, `parse_int` → `Result[Int,
## ParseIntError]`). The lib-decl return type controls cranelift's
## import signature; typeck still consults the hardcoded table in
## `typeck/method_resolvers` for the surface-visible Ty. We declare
## these as the simpler wire-compatible types (`Int` for void* boxed
## returns) and let typeck's own rules drive what the user sees.
